## Rollback

{% if _upgrade_snapshot_full_name is defined %}
If the system is broken post-upgrade, boot a LiveUSB, unlock the LUKS
partitions, import the pool, mount the rolled-back rootfs, and
restore both halves:

```sh
# Roll the ZFS rootfs back to the pre-upgrade snapshot.
zfs rollback {{ _upgrade_snapshot_full_name }}

# /boot is on a separate LUKS partition that ZFS snapshots don't
# cover, so we also need to restore its contents from the tarball
# captured at snapshot time (otherwise the rolled-back rootfs has
# old kernel modules but /boot has the new kernel — they mismatch
# and network/input drivers fail to load on next boot).
#
# Assuming rootfs is mounted at /mnt and /boot is mounted at
# /mnt/boot (adjust paths to match your LiveUSB mount layout):
tar -xpf /mnt{{ _upgrade_boot_tarball }} -C /mnt
```

After both restores, unmount and reboot.
{% else %}
ZFS snapshot was disabled (`upgrade_snapshot_zfs: false`).  Rollback
will require restoring from whatever upstream mechanism you use
(libvirt snapshot, btrfs snapshot, qemu-img diff, etc.).  Note that
/boot is on a separate LUKS partition; if your alternate mechanism
doesn't cover it, you'll hit the same kernel/modules mismatch that
the ZFS-snapshot path solves via the boot tarball.
{% endif %}

